{"summary":"Accept the chemistry-stress consistency amendment because the probe narrative and assertions are currently misaligned; reject the carbonate-equilibrium throttling amendment because the call is already trace-only, the solver is cheap, and reducing cadence would weaken per-tick trace fidelity without evidence of a real bottleneck.","votes":[{"amendment_id":"fr-1-4ed757e6","decision":"accept","rationale":"`probe_chemistry_stress` still documents low-GH + high-NO2 as causing molt failures, but `run_probe_chemistry_stress_with_suffix` no longer asserts any failed-molt signal. In the current code path, the scenario can pass with `molt_failures == 0` and `failed_molt_accum == 0`, so the probe no longer verifies a key part of the stated husbandry story. That mismatch should be resolved by either restoring an appropriate directional assertion or softening the prose to match observed behavior."},{"amendment_id":"fr-1-f7d31042","decision":"reject","rationale":"The performance concern is not well supported by the implementation. `emit_tick_snapshot` already returns unless trace notes are enabled, so this work only happens at `Verbosity::Trace`, and `projected_carbonate_equilibrium()` is a closed-form algebraic solve rather than an iterative heavy routine. Computing it only on daily ticks would make `system:tick_snapshot` less faithful and potentially inconsistent for consumers expecting per-tick pH snapshots, without a demonstrated bottleneck to justify that loss."}]}